<BlogLayout
  title="Mortgage Pre-Approval vs Your Actual Budget: What First-Time Buyers Need to Know | Cliffco Mortgage"
  description="Your mortgage pre-approval amount and your actual homebuying budget are not the same number. Here's what first-time homebuyers need to understand before they start shopping."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/" },
    { name: "Pre-Approval vs Your Actual Budget", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/mortgage-pre-approval-vs-actual-budget-first-time-homebuyer/" },
  ]} />

  <article class="container-prose pt-12 pb-16">

    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Your Pre-Approval Amount Is Not Your Budget: What First-Time Homebuyers Need to Know Before They Start Shopping</h1>

    <ArticleImage />

    <p class="mt-8 font-bold">Before You Fall in Love With a Listing</p>

    <p class="mt-4">If you just got pre-approved for a mortgage and you're ready to start shopping, this is the article to read first. The number on your pre-approval letter is not a recommendation. It's a ceiling, and building your search around it is one of the most common and costly mistakes first-time homebuyers make.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Number Your Lender Gives You and the Number You Should Actually Spend</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A pre-approval tells you the maximum a lender is willing to loan you based on your income, credit, and debt. It says nothing about what you can comfortably afford to live on after the mortgage payment clears every month. Lenders are not financial planners. Their job is to determine risk, not to protect your lifestyle. The pre-approval amount is the top of the range, not the target.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why Debt-to-Income Ratios Allow for Very Tight Budgets</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Most conventional lenders will approve borrowers up to a 43 to 45% debt-to-income ratio. That means nearly half of your gross monthly income can go toward debt payments, including your new mortgage. On paper, that qualifies you. In real life, it can leave very little room for groceries, utilities, childcare, car repairs, retirement contributions, and everything else that makes up an actual monthly budget.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The lender's math doesn't include your gym membership, your student loan payment that's currently deferred, your plans to have a child in two years, or the fact that your current car has 140,000 miles on it. You know those things. The pre-approval letter does not.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How to Find Your Real Number</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The right starting point isn't the pre-approval letter. It's your actual monthly take-home pay and your actual monthly expenses.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Start with what you bring home after taxes and retirement contributions. Then list every fixed expense you carry: car payments, insurance, subscriptions, utilities, phone, food, and anything else that leaves your account every month. Subtract that total from your take-home. What remains is the realistic ceiling for a mortgage payment, and it needs to include principal, interest, taxes, insurance, and any HOA dues, not just the loan payment itself.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Most financial planners suggest keeping total housing costs at or below 28% of gross monthly income. That's a meaningful departure from the 43 to 45% a lender will approve you for. The gap between those two numbers is where buyers get into trouble.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Also account for what changes after you buy. Renters don't pay for a broken water heater or a new roof. Homeowners do. A general rule is to budget 1% of the purchase price annually for maintenance and repairs. On a home priced in the mid-range of most markets, that's a real monthly number that belongs in your planning before you make an offer.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Build your personal budget before you look at listings.</strong> Know your take-home pay, your fixed expenses, and the maximum monthly payment that leaves you comfortable, not just qualified.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculate your all-in payment, not just principal and interest.</strong> Add estimated property taxes, homeowner's insurance, and HOA dues if applicable. That's the real number leaving your account every month.</li>
      <li><strong>Set your search price based on your budget number, not your pre-approval ceiling.</strong> If your comfortable monthly payment translates to a purchase price meaningfully below your pre-approval, shop in that range. The pre-approval is a backstop, not a target.</li>
      <li><strong>Factor in closing costs before you decide how much to put down.</strong> Closing costs typically run 2 to 5% of the purchase price and are due at closing in addition to your down payment. Many first-time buyers underestimate this and arrive at closing underprepared.</li>
      <li><strong>Revisit your budget if your rate changes.</strong> Pre-approvals are typically valid for 60 to 90 days. If rates move during your search, your payment on the same purchase price changes. Rerun the numbers any time your rate environment shifts.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10">See What the Payment Actually Looks Like at Your Number</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Cliffco's Payment Estimator below lets you model the full monthly payment at any purchase price, down payment, and rate combination, including taxes and insurance, so you can see your real number before you fall in love with a listing. Pair that with the <a href="/mortgage-guides/calculators/budget-builder/">Budget Builder</a> to map the payment against your actual monthly expenses and find the price range where you're comfortable, not just approved.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Start there. Then go look at houses.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Find Your Real Number</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Use the Payment Estimator below to model different purchase prices and see the actual monthly payment each one produces. Start lower than your pre-approval ceiling and work up until you find the number that fits your real budget, not just the one the lender approved.</p>

    <!-- Embedded lite Payment Estimator -->
    <div class="mt-12 border border-[var(--color-accent)]/25 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-[var(--color-accent)]/8 px-6 py-5 border-b border-[var(--color-accent)]/15">
        <p class="eyebrow text-xs mb-1">Quick check</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Estimate your monthly payment.</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Enter the numbers for any property to see your estimated monthly payment before you make an offer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Home price ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-price" type="number" value="500000" min="50000" step="5000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Down payment (%)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-down" type="number" value="20" min="3" max="50" step="1" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Interest rate (%)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-rate" type="number" value="7.0" min="1" max="15" step="0.125" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Loan term</label>
            <select id="art-pe-term" class="art-calc-input">
              <option value="30">30-year fixed</option>
              <option value="20">20-year fixed</option>
              <option value="15">15-year fixed</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual taxes ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-taxes" type="number" value="6000" min="0" step="100" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual insurance ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-ins" type="number" value="2400" min="0" step="100" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <button id="art-pe-btn" class="btn btn-primary mt-5">Estimate Payment</button>

        <div id="art-pe-result" class="hidden mt-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-lg">
            <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Principal &amp; Interest</p>
                <p id="art-pe-pi" class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Taxes &amp; Insurance</p>
                <p id="art-pe-ti" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Total Monthly Payment</p>
                <p id="art-pe-total" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p id="art-pe-note" class="mt-3 text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]"></p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-4 p-5 bg-[var(--color-rich-black)] rounded-lg flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold">Know your number before you make an offer.</p>
              <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-1">A Cliffco loan officer can lock a rate and get you pre-approved the same day.</p>
            </div>
            <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-rich-black)] shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap">Talk to a loan officer</a>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-center text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Want to model other scenarios? <a href="/mortgage-guides/calculators/payment-estimator/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] font-medium">View the full payment estimator →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </article>
</BlogLayout>

<style>
  .art-calc-input {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-soft-gray, #d1d5db);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: white;
    outline: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .art-calc-input:focus {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 15%, transparent);
  }
</style>

<script>
  const peBtn = document.getElementById("art-pe-btn")!;
  peBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const price = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-price") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const downPct = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-down") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const rate = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-rate") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const term = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-term") as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const taxes = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-taxes") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const ins = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-ins") as HTMLInputElement).value;

    const loanAmount = price * (1 - downPct / 100);
    const r = rate / 100 / 12;
    const n = term * 12;
    const pi = r === 0 ? loanAmount / n : loanAmount * (r * Math.pow(1 + r, n)) / (Math.pow(1 + r, n) - 1);
    const ti = (taxes + ins) / 12;
    const total = pi + ti;
    const fmt = (v: number) => "$" + Math.round(v).toLocaleString("en-US");

    (document.getElementById("art-pe-pi") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(pi);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-ti") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(ti);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-total") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(total);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-note") as HTMLElement).textContent =
      `Loan amount: ${fmt(loanAmount)} | Down payment: ${fmt(price * downPct / 100)}. Estimate only - does not include PMI, HOA, or other costs.`;
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-result") as HTMLElement).classList.remove("hidden");
  });
</script>
